    "case_summary": "A man in his 60s presented to the emergency department with 5 days of nausea and vomiting. He noted chronic, left-sided abdominal pain and normal passage of stool and flatus. Past medical history was unremarkable. Current medications included only esomeprazole.\n\nVital signs: Afebrile, normotensive, pulse rate 104/minute. Abdominal exam revealed a large, firm, tender mass located near the umbilicus, without rigidity or rebound tenderness.\n\n- Laboratory studies:    - Lactate: 6.3 mmol/L (reference range 0.7-2.2 mmol/L)    - Serial lactate measurements between 4.7-7.2 mmol/L    - Low bicarbonate, low PCO2, marginally acidotic pH on venous blood gas    - Urinalysis and blood cultures showed no evidence of infection      - Imaging studies:    CT Abdomen findings: Diffuse lymphadenopathy and a large mesenteric mass abutting multiple segments of small intestine and encasing the mesenteric vasculature\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Small bowel ischemia due to mesenteric lymphoma\nB. Mesenteric lymphoma with bowel ischemia\nC. Type B lactic acidosis secondary to diffuse large B-cell lymphoma\nD. Non-Hodgkin lymphoma\n",
